                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "When I reviewed Volume 3, I said I hoped that Volume 4 would both shed some light on where this series was headed and that it would at least be longer than two hours. Well, it accomplished that second one... barely. As for the first, this was once again a character-focused story (Chris this time) that saw a new character thrown into the mix. Oh, and there was a mission again where a lot of people got shot. It has become formulaic at this point. Not good.... not good.\n\nHowever, that didn't really bug me too much for this volume. First because I actually was interested despite having a general idea what was going to play out. The storyline may be similar to all the other Phantom Trigger games so far, but it is in execution that Vol. 4 does well. I do have to say the crisis this time got a bit screwy because of the shady ally and the \"plan\" he mentioned for solving it. I guess that was the point though: to show that guy was not only shady as hell, but almost certainly unhinged and sociopathic. Chances are we won't see him again, which leaves yet another side-story aside with some unfinished business. This game sure loves killing off random mooks, but named characters... less so. They should fix that. Seemed to come close this time though. :o\n\nAnyway, if you have come along this far with Phantom Trigger then you know what you are in for. And if you have read my reviews, you also know what I will say next: wait for a sale. There was more game here than usual while keeping the same high quality, but it is still really short. \n\nStill, I admit I am curious what will happen in Vol. 5... if only because Murasaki is probably my favorite character in this crazy cast. I guess we shall see in a few months or so when it comes out. :o\n\nAnnnnd I'm still waiting for my Kickstarter backer rewards from Vol 1/2 to arrive. ;_;",
